The desk merged reports about wrong booking dates into #4471. The subject alone does not
establish a shared cause. The table classifies distinct reports in the export using the
tenant's locale, booking form and channel. It is a snapshot, not the live queue.

## Two problems

- **Parser:** the shared parser reads dates month first even when a tenant uses day-first
  dates. A valid date can silently become a different day and month.
- **Form hint:** booking-v3 tells people to type month first, while its day-first tenant
  forms interpret the entry day first. Give this problem its own engineering ticket.

The desk noted that some reports appeared to describe a one-day move. The table identifies
how many; investigate those symptoms rather than assuming the classification proves their
underlying cause.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## What changed over time

The bars show when reports were received and when they were merged. They describe desk
activity in the export, not a deployment date or proof that either problem is fixed.

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Triage going forward

Compare causes using tenant locale, booking form and channel, and read the report when the
cause is uncertain. Matching subjects can hide different problems; different subjects can
describe the same problem. The accepted subject-only decision remains historical; a new
decision is needed to replace it. Check current queue data before relying on these counts.

